But the concurrent duplicate-insert case: in H2, thread B's insert of same PK while thread A uncommitted → B blocks until A commits, then DuplicateKeyException. B's transaction may be marked for rollback? In Spring, catching the exception inside a @Transactional method doesn't automatically roll back unless the exception propagates; but the DB connection/transaction might be in aborted state (in PostgreSQL it would be; H2 doesn't abort the whole tx on constraint violation). H2 keeps transaction usable. But B already performed its action (e.g., inserted reconciliation) before the idem insert fails... wait order: I do action first, then idem insert. If idem insert fails due to duplicate, B's action effects (e.g., second reconciliation insert) — for reconcile, unique (instruction_id, window_start) would also conflict. Hmm, ordering: better to insert idempotency record FIRST (with response placeholder), then action, then update response? Then concurrent B blocks at insert until A commits → duplicate → B reads A's committed record (but B is in its own tx; A committed so visible) → replay. And B hasn't done any action yet. That's cleaner.

But then the response body must be updated after action: `UPDATE idempotency_record SET response_body=?, http_status=? WHERE ...`. If action fails (exception → rollback), idem row rolls back too. 

Design:
```java
private <T> T executeIdempotent(String op, String requestId, String fingerprint, Class<T> type, Supplier<T> action) {
    Optional<IdemRecord> existing = idemRepo.find(op, requestId);
    if (existing.isPresent()) { check fingerprint; return deserialize; }
    try {
        idemRepo.insertPlaceholder(op, requestId, fingerprint);
    } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
        IdemRecord rec = idemRepo.find(op, requestId).orElseThrow(() -> e);
        check fingerprint; return deserialize(rec);
    }
    T result = action.get();
    idemRepo.complete(op, requestId, serialize(result));
    return result;
}
```

